                ],
                "translations": [
                    {
                        "translation_id": "8213194b-9a4f-401e-9a9a-da80672934eb",
                        "source_entry_id": "760732ac-e3ec-339e-9e72-8f188a992c60",
                        "source_sense_id": "1d253cf2-66fc-386c-a7a6-a9466c971753",
                        "target_lang_word": "qubit",
                        "target_lang_code": "en",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "qubit",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "81afcd22-df34-3514-a930-e82752cbeac6",
                                        "entry_id": "9211976a-e1ef-3f1c-a9b0-677fabed2745",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "A quantum bit; the basic unit of quantum information described by a superposition of two states; a quantum bit in a quantum computer capable of being in a state of superposition; A binary qudit."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "577b524b-7497-43f9-bba0-5fab1b8510cc",
                                                "sense_id": "81afcd22-df34-3514-a930-e82752cbeac6",
                                                "text": "Quantum computing, on the other hand, is based on quantum bits, or qubits."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "9c6774fd-4a65-415f-b38b-8c990717f2f7",
                                                "sense_id": "81afcd22-df34-3514-a930-e82752cbeac6",
                                                "text": "Each extra qubit in a quantum machine doubles the number of simultaneous operations it can perform."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "47305532-e136-4dd6-850d-98daff5a66a7",
                                                "sense_id": "81afcd22-df34-3514-a930-e82752cbeac6",
                                                "text": "Google’s Sycamore computer has all of 53 qubits to its name, as does a new IBM computer, installed online at the company’s Quantum Computation Center in Poughkeepsie, N.Y. System One, IBM’s black cube from tomorrow, only has 20 qubits."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "a5bfec2c-f85c-4514-9c5f-54f72c80fae4",
                                                "sense_id": "81afcd22-df34-3514-a930-e82752cbeac6",
                                                "text": "By harnessing that odd behavior, scientists can instead build a quantum bit, or qubit, which stores a combination of 1 and 0. Two qubits can hold four values at once. And as the number of qubits grows, a quantum computer becomes exponentially more powerful."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "translation_id": "a795e5bc-39c1-468f-a782-fe4b27a9921c",
